\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Edge energy and the positive action}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{For the real [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), take #{t,m>0}
and let #{L_t} be the [weighted Laplacian](fdip-0008). Its
\vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004} is exactly
##{Q_{t,m}=L_t+m^2I.}
Indeed, the displayed array of #{L_t} has diagonal #{1+t} and the
same off-diagonal entries as the action's precision. Adding #{m^2I}
changes that diagonal to #{1+t+m^2}, giving the displayed
#{Q_{t,m}} in \ref{fdip-002P}.}

\p{For #{f=(a,b,c,d)}, the \vocabk{Euclidean quadratic action}{fdip-0004}
is therefore
##{\begin{aligned}
S_{t,m}(f)
 &=\frac12\langle f,L_tf\rangle_V
      +\frac{m^2}{2}\langle f,f\rangle_V\\
 &=\frac12\bigl((b-a)^2+t(c-b)^2\\
 &\qquad\quad +(d-c)^2+t(a-d)^2\bigr)\\
 &\qquad+\frac{m^2}{2}(a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2).
\end{aligned}}
This is the same squared-difference formula as in \ref{fdip-002P}:
half of the edge-once energy plus the mass term. The first summand is
nonnegative, and the mass summand is positive for #{f\ne0}. This
calculation compares the graph convention of
\citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective}
with the positive kinetic sign and half-action convention of
\citet{Section 4, Equation (4.5), p. 3}{wingate2025lattice};
the finite four-cycle equality follows by the substitutions above.}
